  "payload": {
   "provider": "Dr. Michael Best",
   "specialty": "oncology",
   "page_count": 2,
   "pages": [
    {
     "page": 1,
     "text": "**CLINICAL SUMMARY - Moore Kim and Hill Clinic**\n\n**Patient:** Jacob Jacobs \n**DOB:** 03/05/1994 \n**Sex:** M \n**MRN:** 111344\n\n**Encounters:**\n\n**2025-10-21** \n**Reason for Visit:** Cancer (see type table) \n**BP:** 132/89 \n**Weight (lb):** 178\n\nJacob Jacobs, a 31-year-old male, presented for a routine follow-up regarding his stage I melanoma cancer, which he was diagnosed with in October 2019. At the time of his diagnosis, he underwent treatment which has been successful in controlling the cancer. His most recent blood work and imaging studies have not shown any signs of recurrence, and he remains in a stable condition.\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- **BP:** 132/89\n- **Weight:** 178 lbs\n\n**Physical Examination:** \nJacob appeared in no acute distress. His vital signs were stable, and his physical examination was within normal limits. His skin showed no signs of new pigmented lesions or concerning abnormalities.\n\n**Assessment/Plan:**\n- **Melanoma Cancer, Stage I (s/p treatment ~5.6 years ago) - Controlled; meds: none**\n - Jacob’s melanoma has been well-controlled since his treatment approximately 5.6 years ago. His current status is stable, with no evidence of metastatic disease. There is no need for additional medication at this time.\n\n**2024-04-03** \n**Reason for Visit:** Cancer (see type table) \n**BP:** 145/86 \n**Weight (lb):** 170\n\nJacob Jacobs, a 30-year-old male, returned for a routine oncology follow-up His melanoma cancer remains in controlled status following the initial treatment. Recent blood work and imaging have not revealed any recurrence. He continues to be monitored closely.\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- **BP:** 145/86\n- **Weight:** 170 lbs\n\n**Physical Examination:** \nJacob appeared well. His vital signs were stable. Physical examination findings were normal, with no new skin lesions or concerning abnormalities.\n\n**Assessment/Plan:**\n- **Melanoma Cancer, Stage I (s/p treatment ~5.5 years ago) - Controlled; meds: none**\n - Jacob’s melanoma is currently controlled. His treatment has been effective, and there no signs of recurrence. Continued regular follow-up is recommended.\n\n**2022-09-14** \n**Reason for Visit:** Rash \n**BP:** 147/85 \n**Weight (lb):** 174\n\nJacob Jacobs, a 28-year-old male, presented with a rash that was unrelated to his cancer. He was evaluated for the rash, and a diagnosis was made based on clinical findings. There were no signs that the rash was related to his melanoma treatment.\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- **BP:** 147/85\n- **Weight:** 174 lbs\n\n**Physical Examination:** \nJacob presented with a mild rash on his upper arms"
    },
    {
     "page": 2,
     "text": "and chest, which was not concerning for any underlying systemic condition. The rash was treated symptomatically with topical steroids.\n\n**Assessment/Plan:**\n- **Rash - Non-specific** \n - The rash was treated with topical steroids, and Jacob responded well. Follow-up advised to ensure resolution and to monitor for any recurrence.\n\n**Additional Information:**\n\n- **Exercise:** Jacob runs 3-4 times per week, which is a positive lifestyle choice contributing to his overall health.\n- **Social History:** Jacob has a history of tobacco use, having smoked one pack per day starting in 2014. He quit smoking in June 2023, which is a commendable step towards better health. His alcohol consumption is approximately six drinks per week.\n\n**Family History:** \nJacob's father had colon cancer, which resulted in his death at the age of 55. This the importance of regular screenings and preventive measures for cancer in his family.\n\n**Conclusion:** \nJacob Jacobs is a 31-year-old male with a well-controlled stage I melanoma cancer. He is compliant with follow-up appointments and has no signs of recurrence. His overall health is stable, and his lifestyle choices, including regular exercise and quitting smoking, are positive. Regular monitoring and follow-up are essential to maintain his controlled status and overall well-being.\n"
    }
   ],
